{'align_accuracy': nan, 'align_score': 0.015409448184072971, 'attribution_label': 'not attributable', 'citation_links': [], 'claim': 'The limit of resolution of the human eye is 100 micrometers.', 'claim_raw_string': 'The limit of resolution of the human eye is 100 micrometers.', 'error_type': '', 'example_type': 'hard_positive', 'id': 'AttributedQA_0813a8c5-30b9-4f74-9b78-78f51a1af20e', 'label_switch': nan, 'nli_accuracy': nan, 'nli_logit': nan, 'nli_score': 0, 'original_label': nan, 'question': 'what is the limit of resolution of the human eye in micrometers', 'qwen30B_accuracy': nan, 'qwen30B_score': nan, 'qwen4Bnoth_accuracy': nan, 'qwen4Bnoth_score': nan, 'qwen4Bth_accuracy': nan, 'qwen4Bth_score': nan, 'qwen8B_accuracy': nan, 'qwen8B_score': nan, 'references': ['Title: Naked eye
Section: Small objects and maps

Observing a nearby small object without a magnifying glass or a microscope, the size of the object depends on the viewing distance. Under normal lighting conditions (light source ~ 1000 lumens at height 600–700\xa0mm, viewing angle ~ 35 degrees) the angular size recognized by naked eye will be round 1 arc minute = 1/60 degrees = 0.0003 radians. At a viewing distance of 16" = ~ 400\xa0mm, which is considered a normal reading distance in the US, the smallest object resolution will be ~ 0.116\xa0mm. For inspection purposes laboratories use a viewing distance of 200–250\xa0mm, which gives the smallest size of the object recognizable to the naked eye of ~0.058- 0.072\xa0mm (~55-75 micrometers). The accuracy of a measurement ranges from 0.1 to 0.3\xa0mm and depends on the experience of the observer. The latter figure is the usual positional accuracy of faint details in maps and technical plans.'], 'response': 'The limit of resolution of the human eye is 100 micrometers.', 'src_dataset': 'AttributedQA', 'webpage_references': []}

